1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:03,913
Your support assistant just said a pending refund was approved.

2
00:00:04,193 --> 00:00:10,487
Today we'll fix the workflow in Python and see what that tells us about being an AI application engineer.

3
00:00:11,887 --> 00:00:13,423
Welcome to Prompt to Production.

4
00:00:13,703 --> 00:00:17,265
This is lesson one of How to Become an AI Application Engineer.

5
00:00:17,545 --> 00:00:21,172
We'll look at one small support assistant through three different lenses.

6
00:00:21,622 --> 00:00:26,977
First, you can explain how improving a model differs from building the application that uses it.

7
00:00:27,257 --> 00:00:31,779
We'll also talk about where AI assisted prototyping fits into that work.

8
00:00:32,229 --> 00:00:36,560
Second, you'll trace a support ticket through a model draft and an application rule.

9
00:00:36,840 --> 00:00:38,696
The rule answers a practical question.

10
00:00:38,976 --> 00:00:40,427
Where should this draft go next?

11
00:00:40,877 --> 00:00:43,053
Finally, you'll run the same failure twice.

12
00:00:43,333 --> 00:00:45,957
First in a prototype, then with a rule and a test.

13
00:00:46,237 --> 00:00:50,887
You can follow along with plain Python and the example in the course repository.

14
00:00:52,287 --> 00:00:53,290
Start with ownership.

15
00:00:53,570 --> 00:00:56,727
Suppose the support team wants fewer repetitive replies.

16
00:00:57,007 --> 00:00:59,375
We can use a model to draft those replies.

17
00:00:59,655 --> 00:01:06,503
We still have to decide which tickets it touches, where drafts appear, and what happens when it gives us an unusable answer.

18
00:01:06,953 --> 00:01:11,177
Those choices are the AI application engineer's work in this example.

19
00:01:11,457 --> 00:01:15,767
The ML engineer might improve a model that classifies support tickets.

20
00:01:16,047 --> 00:01:24,153
They might curate labeled training data, evaluate a new model on tickets it hasn't seen, and monitor how it performs after deployment.

21
00:01:24,603 --> 00:01:30,641
Vibe coding is a way to explore an idea by describing what you want and trying generated code.

22
00:01:30,921 --> 00:01:34,099
It can get a prototype in front of the support team quickly.

23
00:01:34,379 --> 00:01:37,131
On a small team, one person may do all of this.

24
00:01:37,411 --> 00:01:41,913
The distinction to remember is the outcome each piece of work is trying to improve.

25
00:01:43,313 --> 00:01:44,635
Let's follow one ticket.

26
00:01:44,915 --> 00:01:48,137
A support worker has already tagged it as shipping or refund.

27
00:01:48,417 --> 00:01:50,401
That category comes from the application.

28
00:01:50,681 --> 00:01:52,430
We treat it as an input to the rule.

29
00:01:52,880 --> 00:01:54,480
A model can help with the wording.

30
00:01:54,760 --> 00:01:58,664
For example, a shipping draft might ask the customer for an order number.

31
00:01:58,944 --> 00:02:05,088
A refund case can sound just as polished even when the model has no evidence the refund was approved.

32
00:02:05,538 --> 00:02:09,421
The Python rule decides which output can be shown to staff as a draft.

33
00:02:09,701 --> 00:02:12,431
Here we only want drafts for routine shipping tickets.

34
00:02:12,711 --> 00:02:15,869
For other cases the application requests human review.

35
00:02:16,319 --> 00:02:18,793
Staff make the final decision about what to send.

36
00:02:19,073 --> 00:02:21,484
That gives the prototype a practical boundary.

37
00:02:21,764 --> 00:02:25,348
We can test the routing rule by passing in a category and a draft.

38
00:02:25,628 --> 00:02:28,252
The quality of a draft is a separate evaluation.

39
00:02:29,652 --> 00:02:31,700
The function at the top is our model stub.

40
00:02:31,980 --> 00:02:36,908
A stub is a small substitute that always gives us the same answer for the same input.

41
00:02:37,188 --> 00:02:42,201
That makes the example reproducible and lets you run it with the Python standard library.

42
00:02:42,481 --> 00:02:45,788
The course makes its first live model call in lesson eleven.

43
00:02:46,238 --> 00:02:47,582
Look at the last two lines.

44
00:02:47,862 --> 00:02:50,251
The prototype returns whatever the stub wrote.

45
00:02:50,531 --> 00:02:53,539
That gives us a quick way to see what a draft might look like.

46
00:02:53,819 --> 00:02:58,193
We can also see exactly where the application has to decide how to handle it.

47
00:02:59,593 --> 00:03:01,875
Run the application with the prototype flag.

48
00:03:02,155 --> 00:03:05,377
For this transcript we use the refund example on purpose.

49
00:03:05,657 --> 00:03:09,411
The command prints locally so we can inspect the draft in the terminal.

50
00:03:09,861 --> 00:03:10,779
There's the failure.

51
00:03:11,059 --> 00:03:15,240
The draft says the refund was approved even though this is a pending request.

52
00:03:15,520 --> 00:03:17,376
The stub makes that failure repeatable.

53
00:03:17,656 --> 00:03:23,096
In an application, fluent text like this can make an unverified claim look ready to act on.

54
00:03:23,376 --> 00:03:27,259
We want the application to route this case to staff and leave the draft out.

55
00:03:28,659 --> 00:03:29,853
Start with the category.

56
00:03:30,133 --> 00:03:34,805
Shipping is the only category we've decided to support with drafts in this demo.

57
00:03:35,085 --> 00:03:36,920
Anything else goes to human review.

58
00:03:37,200 --> 00:03:40,293
That includes a category our prototype has never seen.

59
00:03:40,573 --> 00:03:45,501
The empty string means the staff review item carries none of the generated wording.

60
00:03:45,951 --> 00:03:47,381
Then check the draft itself.

61
00:03:47,661 --> 00:03:52,503
A missing value or a string made of spaces would give staff nothing useful to review.

62
00:03:52,783 --> 00:03:56,858
The type check also lets us check the text safely before trying to trim it.

63
00:03:57,138 --> 00:03:59,058
These checks cover the shape of the result.

64
00:03:59,508 --> 00:04:01,748
Finally, return the trimmed draft for staff.

65
00:04:02,028 --> 00:04:06,017
Someone still needs to check the actual order and decide what to send.

66
00:04:06,297 --> 00:04:09,753
Let's run the same ticket through this version and see what changes.

67
00:04:11,153 --> 00:04:12,967
The shipping ticket produces a draft.

68
00:04:13,247 --> 00:04:16,959
The refund ticket still gets that misleading suggestion from the stub.

69
00:04:17,239 --> 00:04:20,887
Now it goes to human review with the generated text withheld.

70
00:04:21,167 --> 00:04:23,919
The unknown category follows the same review path.

71
00:04:24,369 --> 00:04:27,270
Run the tests and you'll see the cases listed by name.

72
00:04:27,550 --> 00:04:29,897
The refund test checks both parts of the result.

73
00:04:30,177 --> 00:04:35,531
It expects the human review destination and an empty string where a draft would otherwise go.

74
00:04:35,981 --> 00:04:37,283
All five tests pass.

75
00:04:37,563 --> 00:04:40,101
We've checked the application rule with fixed inputs.

76
00:04:40,381 --> 00:04:48,701
To judge a real model, we would use a separate set of examples and ask whether each draft matches the order facts and the team's reply guidelines.

77
00:04:48,981 --> 00:04:51,797
Those are different questions, and both are worth testing.

78
00:04:53,197 --> 00:04:56,419
Here are three mistakes worth catching on your next prototype.

79
00:04:56,869 --> 00:04:59,450
The first is testing just the smoothest example.

80
00:04:59,730 --> 00:05:02,333
A routine shipping ticket can make the app look ready.

81
00:05:02,613 --> 00:05:07,370
Try a refund, a new category, and a blank result before you draw that conclusion.

82
00:05:07,820 --> 00:05:11,404
The second is using a model suggestion as if it were approval.

83
00:05:11,684 --> 00:05:13,156
A sentence can sound official.

84
00:05:13,436 --> 00:05:17,852
Use staff or application records to determine whether an action was approved.

85
00:05:18,302 --> 00:05:21,139
The third is asking one test to answer two questions.

86
00:05:21,419 --> 00:05:23,787
Test the routing rule with inputs you control.

87
00:05:24,067 --> 00:05:28,803
Measure draft quality on a set of representative tickets that includes hard cases.

88
00:05:30,203 --> 00:05:32,251
Your homework is to run both versions.

89
00:05:32,531 --> 00:05:35,795
Then add a test for a blank category with a nonempty draft.

90
00:05:36,075 --> 00:05:39,894
Decide what result you expect first, and check that the test agrees.

91
00:05:40,174 --> 00:05:44,142
The example and the run commands are in the repository linked below.

92
00:05:44,592 --> 00:05:50,565
In lesson two we'll draw the modern AI application stack and find where this routing rule belongs.

93
00:05:50,845 --> 00:05:53,811
If you're following the course in order, that's your next lesson.

